1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,000
Welcome.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,150
This is a short overview of GP-RareLink,

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:12,500
a project that reads the published literature on rare genetic diseases with a language model,

4
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:17,650
turns it into structured records, and checks every value against its source.

5
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:26,400
It is funded by Anthropic's AI for Science program and run by the Rare Genomics Institute with the Stanford OVAL lab.

6
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:29,950
You are seeing interim results, for internal review.

7
00:00:30,750 --> 00:00:32,950
Here is the problem.

8
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:36,900
When a child with a rare disease is diagnosed,

9
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:41,650
the responsible variant is often reported once, in a case report,

10
00:00:41,650 --> 00:00:44,800
and never submitted to the public databases.

11
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:49,550
A laboratory testing the next child searches ClinVar and finds nothing.

12
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:51,750
For Alexander disease alone,

13
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:56,500
a 2024 review counts 182 causative variants in 550 published patients,

14
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:01,650
but ClinVar holds 76 pathogenic or likely pathogenic records for the gene.

15
00:01:01,650 --> 00:01:06,800
The knowledge exists; it is scattered across papers no database can query.

16
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:12,750
So the question is whether a language model, reading with strict rules,

17
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:15,100
can do the first pass.

18
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:17,850
Two rules make the answer checkable.

19
00:01:17,850 --> 00:01:23,800
First, every value the model records must carry a verbatim quote from the paper,

20
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:27,750
and a script confirms the quote is really there.

21
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:34,500
Second, the databases we want to fill, ClinVar and OMIM, are never shown to the model.

22
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:38,050
They are held out as the answer key.

23
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:42,800
And the whole analysis plan was frozen before the main run,

24
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:47,550
so what you see is what we said we would report.

25
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:50,550
This is the workflow.

26
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:57,300
One model reader per paper fills a structured form with quotes. A script checks every quote.

27
00:01:57,300 --> 00:02:01,250
A second model pass, which we call the gatekeeper,

28
00:02:01,250 --> 00:02:06,800
re-reads each value against its quote and drops the ones it cannot support.

29
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:11,550
The result is one standard record per individual, a GA4GH phenopacket.

30
00:02:11,550 --> 00:02:16,300
Below the firewall, a separate harness compares the records with ClinVar,

31
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:21,850
OMIM and two published catalogues, and lists the variants the databases are missing.

32
00:02:21,850 --> 00:02:28,600
The orange box is what has not happened yet: the human audit and the expert adjudication.

33
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:34,550
We ran six diseases, chosen to span inheritance modes and organ systems.

34
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:37,300
A20 haploinsufficiency was the development disease,

35
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:42,850
so its numbers are flagged and the other five are the confirmatory set.

36
00:02:42,850 --> 00:02:48,400
Only open-access papers were read, which matters later: for most of these diseases,

37
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:53,150
only a fifth to a third of the literature is open.

38
00:02:53,950 --> 00:02:56,150
The headline numbers.

39
00:02:56,150 --> 00:02:58,900
350 papers became 2,836 individual records,

40
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:03,250
1,793 of them carrying a variant in the disease gene.

41
00:03:03,250 --> 00:03:08,000
Every one of 241,730 quotes was found verbatim in its paper.

42
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,950
466 distinct variants could be placed on the genome.

43
00:03:11,950 --> 00:03:15,900
214 of them are in neither ClinVar nor OMIM.

44
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:21,450
And we recovered 43 of the 75 variants OMIM lists for these diseases.

45
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:27,400
First finding: the receipts hold, but the decisive check has not happened.

46
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:32,950
The gatekeeper judged 0.43 percent of values unsupported across the five confirmatory diseases.

47
00:03:32,950 --> 00:03:35,150
That is the blue.

48
00:03:35,150 --> 00:03:41,500
The orange is what human raters found in the pilot, before the pipeline was frozen:

49
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:43,700
around six percent.

50
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:48,850
The machine's reading of its own errors is far below the humans'.

51
00:03:48,850 --> 00:03:54,800
The pre-registered test is a blinded audit of 250 claims by two panel members.

52
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:59,150
It is built, and nobody has rated a claim yet.

53
00:03:59,150 --> 00:04:02,700
Until then, no accuracy claim can be made.

54
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:07,050
Second finding: we find what we can read.

55
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:11,000
Against OMIM's listed variants we recovered 57 percent overall,

56
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:18,150
from nearly ninety percent for the diseases whose literature is open to eleven percent for Tangier disease,

57
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:22,500
whose variants come from closed papers of the early 2000s.

58
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:29,250
Of the 84 papers OMIM cites as the sources for its variants, we could read 5.

59
00:04:29,250 --> 00:04:35,200
Where we had read the source paper, the variant was usually in our records.

60
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:37,950
Access, not reading, sets the ceiling.

61
00:04:38,750 --> 00:04:41,900
Third finding: the database gap is real.

62
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:45,450
Of 466 genome-placed variants, 245 are in ClinVar,

63
00:04:45,450 --> 00:04:49,800
and 168 of those are classified pathogenic or likely pathogenic.

64
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:54,950
221 are absent from ClinVar; after the checks, 214 remain as candidates,

65
00:04:54,950 --> 00:05:00,500
each with an evidence dossier listing every paper, every quote and every individual.

66
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:06,850
53 more sit in ClinVar as uncertain or conflicting while the papers call them pathogenic.

67
00:05:06,850 --> 00:05:09,600
Nothing is submitted without expert adjudication.

68
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:16,350
Fourth finding: a cheaper open-weight model finds the same variants and loses the receipts.

69
00:05:16,350 --> 00:05:18,550
On identical inputs,

70
00:05:18,550 --> 00:05:26,900
DeepSeek found the same variants about 89 to 95 percent of the time at roughly one 15th of the cost.

71
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:33,250
But it left 3.0 to 3.6 percent of its values with no quote at all,

72
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:38,000
and the gatekeeper rejected 6.14 to 8.64 percent of its claims,

73
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,350
against Claude's 0.20 to 0.38.

74
00:05:41,150 --> 00:05:43,350
Two more checks.

75
00:05:43,350 --> 00:05:55,700
Running a tenth of the papers twice gave the same checklist answers 97 to 100 percent of the time and the same variants 83 to 100 percent of the time.

76
00:05:55,700 --> 00:06:04,850
And a memorization probe on 180 papers found no sign that either model recalled the papers from training rather than reading them.

77
00:06:05,650 --> 00:06:07,850
What does this mean?

78
00:06:07,850 --> 00:06:11,800
On accuracy: the pipeline produces records with complete provenance,

79
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:16,550
but a human-rated error rate is the one number it lacks,

80
00:06:16,550 --> 00:06:21,700
and the audit that provides it costs no model time. On coverage:

81
00:06:21,700 --> 00:06:29,250
a decision on access to paywalled papers would change these results more than any change to the model.

82
00:06:29,250 --> 00:06:34,400
On the answer key: databases curated by people deserve the same scrutiny;

83
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:37,550
OMIM caught one of our normalization errors,

84
00:06:37,550 --> 00:06:42,700
and we found two OMIM entries linked to the wrong ClinVar records.

85
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:46,250
This is where you come in.

86
00:06:46,250 --> 00:06:52,200
We need two raters for the 250-claim audit, roughly ten to fifteen hours each,

87
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:54,400
and a tie-breaker.

88
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:59,150
We need experts to adjudicate the 214 candidates from their dossiers.

89
00:06:59,150 --> 00:07:03,900
We need people to fill four comparator sheets from natural-history cohorts.

90
00:07:03,900 --> 00:07:08,250
And we need reviewers for the methods and the paper.

91
00:07:08,250 --> 00:07:17,000
The dashboard has a short form where you can tell us who you are and how you would like to contribute.

92
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:20,000
Thank you.

93
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:25,950
Everything in this overview, the papers, the deck, the candidate list and the form,

94
00:07:25,950 --> 00:07:28,150
is on the dashboard.

95
00:07:28,150 --> 00:07:32,500
The numbers all trace to files in the project repository,

96
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:36,850
and the report says plainly which questions are still open.

97
00:07:36,850 --> 00:07:40,000
We look forward to working with you.
